quickAnswer: "Paste your code and press Format. The Ruff formatter rewrites it in Black-compatible style: 4-space indents, spaces around operators, two blank lines between top-level functions and classes, double quotes, and lines wrapped at 88 characters (or 79 for strict PEP 8). It changes only layout, not behaviour, and reports syntax errors with a line number instead of guessing a fix."
workedExamples:
  - title: "A cramped function"
    before: |-
      def  calculate_total( items,tax_rate = 0.18 ) :
        subtotal=sum( item['price']*item['qty'] for item in items )
        return subtotal*( 1+tax_rate )
    after: |-
      def calculate_total(items, tax_rate=0.18):
          subtotal = sum(item["price"] * item["qty"] for item in items)
          return subtotal * (1 + tax_rate)
    result: "Spaces around = and * are normalised, the default argument loses its spaces, the body is indented with 4 spaces, and single quotes become double quotes."
  - title: "A long dictionary"
    before: |-
      config = {'host':'localhost','port':5432,'database':'inventory','user':'admin','options':{'sslmode':'require','connect_timeout':10}}
    after: |-
      config = {
          "host": "localhost",
          "port": 5432,
          "database": "inventory",
          "user": "admin",
          "options": {"sslmode": "require", "connect_timeout": 10},
      }
    result: "The line is longer than 88 characters, so each key moves to its own line with a trailing comma. The nested dictionary still fits on one line, so it stays there."
  - title: "A class with crowded methods"
    before: |-
      class   Account :
          def __init__(self,owner,balance=0):
              self.owner=owner;self.balance=balance
          def deposit(self,amount):
              if amount<=0: raise ValueError('Deposit must be positive')
              self.balance+=amount
              return self.balance
    after: |-
      class Account:
          def __init__(self, owner, balance=0):
              self.owner = owner
              self.balance = balance

          def deposit(self, amount):
              if amount <= 0:
                  raise ValueError("Deposit must be positive")
              self.balance += amount
              return self.balance
    result: "The two statements joined by a semicolon are split onto separate lines, the one-line if body is expanded, and a blank line separates the methods."
commonMistakes:
  - title: "Mixing tabs and spaces"
    description: "Python treats a tab and spaces as different indentation, so a block that mixes them raises IndentationError or TabError. The formatter cannot parse such code and reports \"Unexpected indentation\" with a line number. Re-indent that block with spaces only, then format again."
  - title: "Expecting the formatter to fix syntax errors"
    description: "A missing colon, bracket or quote makes the file unparseable. The formatter only rearranges valid code, so fix the error at the reported line first. The line it reports can be just after the real mistake, such as the line following an unclosed bracket."
  - title: "Expecting imports to be sorted or split"
    description: "Formatting keeps import os, sys as written. Splitting one import per line and sorting imports are linter jobs, done by Ruff's lint rules or isort, not by the formatter."
  - title: "Not agreeing on a line length"
    description: "If one person formats at 79 columns and another at 88, every file changes back and forth. Pick one value for the project and keep it in a shared configuration, such as line-length in pyproject.toml."
  - title: "Leaving a trailing comma you do not want"
    description: "A trailing comma after the last item tells the formatter to keep a call or collection expanded one item per line, even if it would fit on one line. Remove the comma if you want it collapsed."
faqs:
  - q: "How do I format Python code online?"
    a: "Paste your code into the box, choose a line length, indentation and quote style, and press Format or Ctrl+Enter. The formatted code appears below, ready to copy. The formatter runs in your browser, so your code is not uploaded."
  - q: "Is this the same as Black?"
    a: "It uses the Ruff formatter, which is designed as a drop-in replacement for Black and produces near-identical output on Black-formatted code. Unlike Black, Ruff lets you choose the quote style."
  - q: "What line length does PEP 8 recommend?"
    a: "PEP 8 recommends a maximum of 79 characters for code and 72 for comments and docstrings, and allows teams to agree on up to 99. Black and Ruff default to 88, which is why 88 is the default here."
  - q: "Why does the formatter say \"Unexpected indentation\"?"
    a: "A line is indented more than the block it belongs to, often because tabs and spaces are mixed or a line was pasted with extra indentation. Fix the indentation at the reported line so each block uses the same number of spaces."
  - q: "Does the formatter change single quotes to double quotes?"
    a: "Yes, by default, to match Black. Choose Single to prefer single quotes, or Preserve to keep each string's existing quotes. Quotes are only swapped when that does not require extra escaping."
  - q: "Will formatting change how my code runs?"
    a: "No. The formatter only changes whitespace, line breaks, quotes and parentheses that do not affect meaning. If the code cannot be parsed, it stops with an error rather than changing anything."
  - q: "What is the difference between a formatter and a linter?"
    a: "A formatter rewrites layout automatically. A linter reports problems such as unused imports, undefined names or risky patterns, and only some of those can be fixed automatically. Most projects use both."
  - q: "Is my code uploaded to a server?"
    a: "No. The Ruff formatter is compiled to WebAssembly and served from this site; it downloads the first time you format and then runs inside your browser."
