    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="pep8-heading">
      <h2 id="pep8-heading">PEP 8 basics</h2>
      <p>PEP 8 is Python's official style guide. A formatter applies the mechanical parts of it for you, so you can spend review time on what the code does rather than how it is laid out. The rules it enforces most often are:</p>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>Four spaces per indentation level.</strong> Spaces, not tabs, and never a mix of the two in one block.</li>
        <li><strong>A limited line length.</strong> PEP 8 recommends 79 characters for code and 72 for comments and docstrings, so that two files fit side by side and diffs stay readable.</li>
        <li><strong>Blank lines with meaning.</strong> Two blank lines around top-level functions and classes, one blank line between methods inside a class.</li>
        <li><strong>Spaces around operators.</strong> One space either side of assignment and comparison operators (<code>x = y + 1</code>, <code>if total &gt;= limit</code>), but no spaces around <code>=</code> in keyword arguments and defaults (<code>def f(rate=0.18)</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>No extra spaces inside brackets</strong> and one statement per line, so <code>a = 1; b = 2</code> becomes two lines.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Naming conventions, such as <code>snake_case</code> for functions and variables and <code>CapWords</code> for classes, are also part of PEP 8, but a formatter never renames anything. That is a job for a linter and for code review.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="ruff-style-heading">
      <h2 id="ruff-style-heading">How Ruff and Black style differs from plain PEP 8</h2>
      <p>PEP 8 leaves many choices open, and two people following it can still lay out the same code differently. Black, and the Ruff formatter that copies its style, remove those choices so every file in a project looks the same:</p>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>88 columns instead of 79.</strong> Black chose 88 because it produces noticeably shorter files than 79 while still fitting comfortably on screen. Choose 79 here if your project follows PEP 8's limit strictly.</li>
        <li><strong>Double quotes by default.</strong> PEP 8 allows either quote style as long as you are consistent; Black standardises on double quotes. Ruff adds the option to prefer single quotes or to keep existing ones.</li>
        <li><strong>Predictable wrapping.</strong> When a call, list or dictionary is too long, it is split one item per line with a trailing comma, so adding an item later changes only one line in the diff.</li>
        <li><strong>The magic trailing comma.</strong> If you leave a trailing comma after the last item, the formatter keeps the expression expanded even when it would fit on one line.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Because the output is the same no matter who runs it, teams usually format on save or in a pre-commit hook and stop discussing layout in reviews.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="cannot-fix-heading">
      <h2 id="cannot-fix-heading">Errors the formatter cannot fix</h2>
      <p>The formatter first parses your code into a syntax tree, and only then prints it back out in a consistent style. If parsing fails, there is nothing to format, so it reports an error with the line and column instead of guessing what you meant:</p>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>IndentationError and TabError.</strong> Mixing tabs and spaces, or indenting a line more than its block, makes the structure ambiguous. The formatter reports "Unexpected indentation" at the first such line.</li>
        <li><strong>Syntax errors.</strong> A missing colon after <code>def</code> or <code>if</code>, an unclosed bracket or an unterminated string stops parsing. An unclosed bracket is often reported on the next line, because that is where Python realises the expression did not end.</li>
        <li><strong>Problems a linter finds.</strong> Unused imports, undefined names or a shadowed variable are valid syntax, so the formatter leaves them alone.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>When the formatter reports an error, the cursor jumps to that position in the input so you can fix it and format again.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="privacy-heading">
      <h2 id="privacy-heading">Private by design</h2>
